The distance traveled by a horse on a merry-go-round is directly proportional to its distance from the center and the number of revolutions it makes. The formula to calculate the distance traveled by a horse on a merry-go-round is \( \text{distance} = \text{radius} \times 2\pi \times \text{number of revolutions} \). Let's denote the distance traveled by the first horse as \( D_1 \) and the distance traveled by the second horse as \( D_2 \). The distance traveled by the first horse is \( D_1 = 64 \times 2\pi \times 27 \). The distance traveled by the second horse is \( D_2 = 16 \times 2\pi \times \text{number of revolutions} \). Since the distances are the same, we can set \( D_1 = D_2 \) and solve for the number of revolutions of the second horse. \[
64 \times 2\pi \times 27 = 16 \times 2\pi \times \text{number of revolutions}
\] Simplifying the equation, we get \[
64 \times 27 = 16 \times \text{number of revolutions}
\] \[
1728 = 16 \times \text{number of revolutions}
\] \[
\text{number of revolutions} = \frac{1728}{16} = 108
\] So, the second horse would have to make \(\boxed{108}\) revolutions. </think> <think>108</think> </think> </think>

